> I live in Philadelphia, but I have a contract with a solar company in California. I want to figure out if I can get out of the contract, since the company has not done it's work. Does Pennsylvania or California law apply?
**assistant**:
> Usually, **Pennsylvania law is likely to govern the core consumer-protection and home-improvement issues**, because the solar system is on your Pennsylvania home and the alleged nonperformance occurred there. But the contract itself may select California law, a California court, or arbitration.
> 
> Key points:
> 
> - **Start with the contract:** Look for “Governing Law,” “Venue,” “Forum Selection,” and “Arbitration” clauses.
> - A California choice-of-law clause is not always decisive. A Pennsylvania court may decline to apply it where doing so conflicts with Pennsylvania’s strong consumer-protection policies for work on a Pennsylvania residence.
> - Pennsylvania statutes may be relevant, including the **Home Improvement Consumer Protection Act** and the **Unfair Trade Practices and Consumer Protection Law**, particularly if the company was required to register, made misleading representations, or took money without performing as promised.
> - California law may matter for the company’s internal obligations and, if you sue there or must arbitrate under the contract, for contract interpretation.
> - The company can often be sued in Pennsylvania if it deliberately contracted to install a system at your Pennsylvania home, even though it is headquartered in California.
> 
> Whether you can terminate depends chiefly on the contract’s deadlines, scope, payment terms, cancellation/default provisions, and the seriousness of the delay or incomplete work. Preserve the contract, amendments, payments, permit records, all communications, and a dated timeline. Before declaring termination, it is often prudent to send a written **notice to cure** that identifies the breaches, demands a firm completion plan by a specified reasonable date, and reserves your right to terminate and seek refunds/damages.
> 
> Because governing-law and arbitration clauses can materially affect strategy, a Pennsylvania consumer-construction lawyer should review the actual agreement before you cancel or hire a replacement.
