> Yes, that is a sensible understanding.
> 
> A **Pennsylvania consumer/construction lawyer** is the right first call because the project, property, alleged nonperformance, and likely evidence are all in Pennsylvania. They can assess:
> 
> - whether the company complied with Pennsylvania requirements for residential solar/home-improvement work;
> - whether its delay or failures justify termination, withholding further payment, or a refund;
> - whether and how the California venue/law clauses are likely to be enforced;
> - the risk that the company could pursue cancellation fees in Los Angeles; and
> - the safest wording and procedure for a notice to cure or termination letter.
> 
> Do not simply stop payment or announce cancellation without reviewing the contract’s notice, cure, and fee provisions. A lawyer can help you create a record showing that the company, rather than you, materially breached first—often the best leverage for resolving this without litigation.
> 
> You could also look for a Pennsylvania lawyer who handles **consumer protection, home-improvement disputes, or solar-contract disputes**, rather than only general real estate law.
